# Explore before you debug

Before proposing fixes, editing code, or answering architecture questions, orient yourself in this repo.

## Required first pass (every task)

1. Read `README.md` and `AGENTS.md` if not already in context.
2. List the top-level layout (`CMakeLists.txt`, `src/`, `src/shaders/`).
3. Identify the entry point and main control flow before diving into a single file.
4. Search for symbols related to the bug or feature (`grep` / codebase search) instead of guessing file locations.

## For this project (SpaceInvaders)

| Area | Path | Role |
|------|------|------|
| Entry + game loop | `src/main.cpp` | GLFW window, GLAD init, delta-time loop |
| Game logic | `src/Game.cpp`, `src/Game.h` | Input, enemies, bullets, collisions, win/lose |
| Shaders | `src/Shader.cpp`, `src/shaders/*.glsl` | GLSL compile/link, uniforms |
| Build | `CMakeLists.txt` | FetchContent (GLFW, GLAD), target `SpaceInvaders` |

**Runtime flow:** `main()` → parse CLI color → GLFW/GLAD init → `Game` ctor → loop (`processInput` → `update` → `render`) until ESC or `game.hasEnded()`.

## Before changing code

- State in one short paragraph what you think is happening and which files own that behavior.
- Prefer reading call sites and data flow over patching the first file that mentions an error.
- For build/runtime issues on cloud/CI, follow the working recipe in `AGENTS.md` (do not assume stock `cmake -S . -B build` works).

## When debugging

1. Reproduce or read the exact error message.
2. Trace from entry point to the failing subsystem.
3. Check build vs runtime vs environment (OpenGL, GLFW, headless/Xvfb) before editing source.

Do not skip this orientation step, even for small-looking bugs.
